# mokuro-bridge configuration - copy to .env and adapt, or export these.
# The server does NOT read .env automatically; either `set -a && source .env
# && set +a` before ./run.sh, or export the values in your launcher/shell.

# ── Server ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keep the default 127.0.0.1 - the bridge is unauthenticated, so loopback is
# the only access control it has.
## Extra roots the local-ingest routes may read from. Comma-separated, absolute
# paths only; a relative entry is ignored. The defaults are the home directory
# and the platform temp locations, so this is only needed for a library kept
# somewhere else, a second drive on Windows for example. Relative entries are
# refused rather than resolved against the working directory.
MOKURO_BRIDGE_INGEST_ROOTS=

MOKURO_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_PORT=62642   # 62642 spells "MANGA" on a phone keypad

# On an interactive first run with no remote account, the bridge opens the
# setup page in the default browser. Set 0 to stop that; same as --no-browser.
# A service started by launchd or systemd has no terminal, so it never opens
# a browser either.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_OPEN_BROWSER=1
# Print the technical startup lines (paths, engine, fetch proxy) in addition
# to the short plain-language banner; same as --verbose.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_VERBOSE=1

# Origins allowed to POST from browser userscripts (comma separated).
# Default: * (any origin). The bridge is loopback-only and sends no cookies,
# so it is unauthenticated by design; this variable is the opt-in way to
# narrow it back to a list. Uncomment to allow only the viewers you use:
#CORS_ORIGINS=https://viewer.bookwalker.jp,https://viewer-trial.bookwalker.jp

# ── Paths ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scratch space for pages + OCR JSON while a volume is in progress.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_WORK_DIR=/Users/you/mokuro-input

# Where finished <series>/<volume>.{cbz,mokuro,webp} land when not uploading.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_OUTPUT_DIR=/Users/you/mokuro-bridge/output

# Base URL `mokuro-bridge-ocr` (the folder-OCR companion) uses to reach a
# running bridge. Match it to MOKURO_BRIDGE_HOST/PORT above.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:62642

# ── MEGA upload (optional - off by default) ─────────────────────────────
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPLOAD_DEFAULT=true        # finalize uploads unless told otherwise
                                         # (also accepts an account alias: mega-work)
#MEGA_LIBRARY_ROOT=/Root/mokuro-reader
#MEGA_EMAIL=you@example.com               # credentials (alternative to the
#MEGA_PASSWORD=super-secret               #  dashboard's Add destination or
                                         #  `mokuro-bridge --setup-upload mega`)
#MEGA_CREDS_FILE=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/credentials.env

# ── Extra accounts and aliases (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# Every provider can hold any number of accounts. Add them from the dashboard
# (Add destination) or from the terminal with `mokuro-bridge --setup-upload
# mega --name work --root /Root/other-library --label "Work"`. Each account
# gets an alias, which is the id shown in the dashboard, `--list-uploads`,
# /health and /upload-methods: the first account of a provider is the bare
# provider name (mega, drive, onedrive, webdav, rclone), later ones are mega-2, mega-3, or a
# name derived from --name (mega:work becomes mega-work). Scripts pass the
# alias as upload_method; the old `mega:work` spelling keeps resolving.
# Non-secret metadata lives in this directory (secrets stay in the keychain
# or their own 0600 files):
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_ACCOUNTS_DIR=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/accounts

# ── Google Drive upload (optional; needs rclone, or the native Google libraries)
# Add an account from the dashboard's Add destination, which opens Google's
# sign-in in a new tab, or run `mokuro-bridge --setup-upload drive` in the
# terminal. With rclone installed the page signs in through rclone, which keeps
# the token itself and needs no Google project; these overrides are for the
# native sign-in, where a free Google Cloud "Desktop app" OAuth client is
# needed once. The dashboard lets you paste its ID and secret for that sign-in
# only, and the secret is never stored. Set them here instead to skip the paste.
# Optional overrides:
#DRIVE_ROOT_NAME=mokuro-reader            # library folder at My Drive root
#DRIVE_CREDS_FILE=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/drive_credentials.json
#DRIVE_CLIENT_ID=1234-....apps.googleusercontent.com   # skip the paste step
#DRIVE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-....                         # ...and the secret
#DRIVE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/Users/you/client_secret.json   # or a full client_secrets.json

# ── OneDrive upload (optional; needs rclone, or the msal library)
# The dashboard's OneDrive sign-in uses Microsoft's device-code flow, which
# needs an Azure app registration (public client) and the variables below.
# The sign-in needs one free Azure application (client) ID, the same model
# upstream mokuro-reader uses. The dashboard's OneDrive form collects it and
# saves it here, so you normally do not set it by hand; when this variable is
# set in the service environment it pins the value and the page will not
# overwrite it. With rclone installed the page signs in through rclone
# instead, which brings its own Microsoft app and needs no Azure at all.
#ONEDRIVE_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000   # Azure app (public client)
#ONEDRIVE_APP_FILE=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/onedrive_app.json
#ONEDRIVE_ROOT_NAME=mokuro-reader         # library folder at your OneDrive root
#ONEDRIVE_TOKEN_FILE=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/onedrive_token.json

# ── WebDAV upload (optional; needs requests, or rclone)
# Any WebDAV server works: Nextcloud, ownCloud, Synology, a NAS or a folder
# behind a reverse proxy. Add an account from the dashboard's Add destination
# (server URL + username + password), which contacts the server once before
# saving, or run `mokuro-bridge --setup-upload webdav`. With rclone installed
# the page signs in through rclone instead and stores the password in rclone's
# own config. In the native flow the URL and username are stored with the
# account and the password goes to a 0600 file (plus the keychain for the first
# account). These variables seed the FIRST account only, so an existing
# single-account setup keeps working:
#WEBDAV_BASE_URL=https://cloud.example.com/remote.php/dav/files/you/
#WEBDAV_USERNAME=you
#WEBDAV_PASSWORD=super-secret
#WEBDAV_ROOT_NAME=mokuro-reader          # folder created under the base URL
#WEBDAV_CREDS_FILE=/Users/you/.config/mokuro-bridge/webdav_credentials.json
# A self-signed certificate is accepted per account from the dashboard's
# WebDAV form (Advanced settings), not from an environment variable.

# ── rclone (optional; needs the rclone binary) ─────────────────────────
# A destination for anything rclone can reach, including OneDrive without
# an Azure app registration: rclone ships its own Microsoft app ID, so a
# personal Microsoft account is enough. Install rclone and the dashboard can
# run the sign-in for MEGA, Google Drive, OneDrive or WebDAV, or use a remote
# you already made with `rclone config` (for OneDrive choose Microsoft OneDrive
# and leave the client ID blank). The token stays in rclone's own
# configuration; the bridge stores no credential for it.
#RCLONE_ROOT_NAME=mokuro-reader         # folder created on the remote
#RCLONE_BINARY=/usr/local/bin/rclone    # when the service PATH lacks rclone

# ── OCR engine ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to a mokuro checkout instead of the installed package; either engine
# works, and the faster fork is the one this project recommends.
#MOKURO_REPO=/Users/you/mokuro
# Pages the OCR worker takes in one batch. Defaults to max(OCR_CHUNK_SIZE, 32).
#OCR_BATCH_SIZE=32                  # also on the dashboard's Preferences (Reading pages)
#OCR_CHUNK_SIZE=8
#OCR_IDLE_FLUSH_S=1.5
# One line at startup when the loaded engine is the slower upstream build.
# Set 0 to silence it; this is separate from MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPDATE_CHECK.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_MOKURO_NOTICE=1
# Round-robin mixed-volume batches; set 0 for legacy FIFO/finalize priority.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_OCR_FAIR_SCHEDULING=true
# Fraction of a mixed batch reserved for sessions waiting on finalize (0-1).
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_OCR_FINALIZE_PRIORITY=0.5
# Pipeline worker processes the mokuro fork starts for itself. The bridge drives
# OCR on its own single worker thread, so 0 (the default) means one active OCR
# thread on the machine and no idle model copies in child processes.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_OCR_WORKERS=0

# ── Independent work lanes ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Health, page I/O, finalize and upload each run on their own bounded thread
# pool, so one slow class cannot starve another. Raise a lane on a big machine;
# lower it on a small one. All values must be at least 1.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_CONTROL_THREADS=4
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_DATA_THREADS=8
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_FINALIZE_THREADS=4
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPLOAD_THREADS=8
# Refuse a remote upload when a volume has fewer pages than this, which catches
# a failed scrape that captured only junk pages. Default 1 (effectively off).
#MIN_PAGES_FOR_MEGA=10

# ── Page-fetch accelerator ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Extra localhost ports the downloader can fetch pages through. Chrome allows 6
# concurrent connections per origin, and an origin includes the port, so each
# port here is worth 6 more sockets to the browser. 0 disables the feature.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_FETCH_PORTS=48
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_FETCH_CONCURRENCY=0        # 0 = no cap; also on the dashboard's Preferences
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_FETCH_UPSTREAM=https://bw-bv-epubs.bookwalker.jp
# Comma-separated host patterns the accelerator may forward to. Empty/unset
# means "*", i.e. any public host: the real gate is the address check, which
# refuses loopback, private, link-local, multicast and reserved targets (so LAN
# hosts and 169.254.169.254 are blocked) but allows the public internet. Set it
# to pin the accelerator to specific CDNs.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_FETCH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=bw-bv-epubs.bookwalker.jp

# ── Update check ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bridge asks the public GitHub releases API whether a newer version
# exists, and reports it in /health and via `--check-update`. Nothing is ever
# downloaded or installed automatically. Set 0 on an offline machine; a
# `--check-update` you run by hand still works then.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPDATE_CHECK=true      # also a switch in the dashboard's Preferences
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPDATE_TTL_S=21600          # reuse a result for 6 hours
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_UPDATE_TIMEOUT_S=5

# ── Dashboard settings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The configuration dashboard (http://127.0.0.1:62642/) writes the values it
# edits here, and every reader prefers an environment variable over the file.
# A row whose variable is set here is shown as pinned and is skipped on save,
# so exporting a value never lets a page overwrite the stored one. The page
# validates the output folder as an absolute path and the read roots as
# absolute, comma-separated paths. See docs/dashboard.md.
#MOKURO_BRIDGE_SETTINGS_FILE=~/.config/mokuro-bridge/settings.json

# ── Dev only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#UVICORN_RELOAD=1
# Optional GitHub token for `--check-series-reference` and
# `--refresh-series-reference` API calls; raises the anonymous rate limit.
#GITHUB_TOKEN=
